# CROSS-PLATFORM LINE ENDINGS
#
# This is the single most important file in a mixed macOS/Windows repository,
# and it has to exist BEFORE the first Windows clone.
#
# THE PROBLEM
#
# Windows uses CRLF (\r\n) line endings; macOS, Linux, and every container
# use LF (\n). Git on Windows defaults to converting LF to CRLF on checkout.
# That is harmless for Python source -- but it is catastrophic for anything
# executed by a shell.
#
# A shell script with CRLF line endings fails inside a Linux container with:
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#     bash\r: command not found
#
# The `^M` is the stray carriage return. The error names a file that plainly
# exists, which sends people hunting for missing packages and broken paths
# for an hour. It is one of the most confusing errors in cross-platform work
# and it is entirely preventable by this file.
#
# `* text=auto` lets Git normalize text files to LF in the repository while
# still checking out CRLF on Windows where that is appropriate. The explicit
# `eol=lf` rules below force LF on disk everywhere, for the files where CRLF
# would break execution.

* text=auto

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MUST be LF everywhere -- these are executed by a shell, in a container, or
# by a Linux CI runner
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf
*.mk            text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

# YAML is parsed by tools that mostly tolerate CRLF, but block scalars (the
# `run: |` steps in a workflow) carry their line endings into the shell --
# so a CRLF workflow file produces the same `\r` failures inside a run step.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Python: LF everywhere. Consistent diffs, and these files are copied into
# Linux containers.
*.py            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
*.ini           text eol=lf
requirements*.txt text eol=lf

# Documentation
*.md            text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows-native files -- CRLF is correct here
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary -- never touch these
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.gz            binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
